        <div id="model-overview" class="content-card">
          <div class="content-text-frame">
            <h2>Overview</h2>

            <p><strong>Executive Summary</strong></p>
            <p>The core challenge of EchoYeast is to rapidly and reproducibly screen, from 17 nanobody sequences,
              the recognition element with the highest specificity for H1N1 HA and the lowest cross-reactivity risk
              in the absence of experimental structures and wet-lab screening conditions, and to further validate the
              molecular mechanisms of two gating systems (hM1Dq and Ste2p).</p>
            <p>We established a computational-first / model-guided multiscale molecular docking and simulation
              pipeline:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Structure prediction using AlphaFold2/ColabFold;</li>
              <li>Initial screening by multi-antigen rigid docking with ClusPro;</li>
              <li>Semi-flexible refinement with HADDOCK 2.5;</li>
              <li>Interface analysis with PyMOL + SPICE;</li>
              <li>100 ns MD with GROMACS;</li>
              <li>Binding free energy calculation and per-residue decomposition with gmx_MMPBSA;</li>
              <li>Positive control redocking with 9VH4;</li>
              <li>Parallel modeling of the Path A (hM1Dq) and Path B (Ste2p) gating systems.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>By combining multiple orthogonal computational methods, we reduced the 17-candidate nanobody pool to
              a single lead (R1aB6) and identified a testable mutational hypothesis (R31A), providing a prioritized
              experimental roadmap rather than a replacement for wet-lab validation.</p>

            <p>Key conclusions:</p>
            <table style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.table }}">
              <thead>
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.headRow }}">
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Item</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Conclusion</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {% for row in modelDocking.display.executiveSummary %}
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.row }}">
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.item }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.conclusion }}</td>
                </tr>
                {% endfor %}
              </tbody>
            </table>

            <p><strong>Important Statement:</strong> All computational data on this page are derived from molecular
              modeling and have not yet been validated by wet-lab experiments. The ultimate value of the model lies
              in providing testable hypotheses (the ARG-B:31 hotspot and the R31A mutant) and prioritization for
              subsequent experiments.</p>

            <p><strong>1. Motivation and Design Need</strong></p>
            <p>EchoYeast requires a nanobody that specifically recognizes the hemagglutinin (HA) of influenza A
              (H1N1) virus with minimal cross-reactivity. We have 17 candidate nanobody sequences, but none of them
              have experimental structures in the PDB; moreover, screening them one by one by wet-lab experiments is
              both time-consuming and expensive.</p>
            <p>Therefore, the model must answer the following scientific questions:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Among the 17 candidate nanobodies, which one has the optimal binding ability and specificity for
                H1N1 HA?</li>
              <li>What is the binding mode between this nanobody and H1N1 HA? What are the key hotspot residues?</li>
              <li>In the two gating systems hM1Dq and Ste2p, how do MT1/MT1_5 bind the receptor? How does Gpa1
                couple?</li>
              <li>Which candidates should be prioritized for wet-lab experiments? Which should be eliminated?</li>
            </ul>
            <p>We adopt a computational-first / model-guided strategy, using multiscale computational models to guide
              experimental design rather than replace experiments.</p>

            <p><strong>2. System Overview and Modeling Logic</strong></p>
            <p><strong>2.1 Three main lines</strong></p>
            <p>Part 1 (Common Foundation): HA &times; nanobody docking &mdash; screening R1aB6<br>
              &#9500;&#9472;&#9472; Path A (hM1Dq gating system): hM1Dq + MT1 + humanized Gpa1<br>
              &#9492;&#9472;&#9472; Path B (Ste2p gating system): Ste2p + MT1_5 + Gpa1</p>

            <figure style="margin:1.5rem 0; text-align:center;">
              <img src="../static/image/dry-lab/model/model-fig-2-1-pipeline.png" alt="Figure 1. Overall EchoYeast modelling pipeline: 17 nanobody sequences are structure-predicted with AlphaFold2/ColabFold 1.6.2 and docked by ClusPro against 6 antigens; the top 5 candidates are refined with HADDOCK 2.5, analysed with PyMOL + SPICE, and checked with 100 ns MD and MM-GBSA against the 9VH4 positive control; Part 2 runs the same pipeline on the hM1Dq and Ste2p gating systems" loading="lazy" width="1268" height="762" style="max-width:100%; height:auto; border:1px solid #ddd;">
              <figcaption style="margin-top:.5rem; color:#666; font-size:.9rem;">Figure 1. Overall modelling pipeline: Part 1 screens the recognition element (17 nanobodies → R1aB6); Part 2 validates the two gating systems at atomic level (computational predictions, not experimental results)</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p><strong>2.2 Logic Chain</strong></p>
            <p>Question (Which nanobody is optimal?) &rarr; Hypothesis (rigid + semi-flexible + MD can converge
              candidates [AMBIGUOUS]) &rarr; Methods (ClusPro &rarr; HADDOCK &rarr; PyMOL/SPICE &rarr; 100 ns MD
              &rarr; MM-GBSA) &rarr; Outputs (specificity index / HADDOCK score / interface residues / RMSD /
              &Delta;G_bind / hotspot residues) &rarr; Validation (positive control redocking + multi-method
              cross-validation) &rarr; Design decision (select R1aB6, propose an R31A mutant).</p>

            <p><strong>2.3 Seven-Stage Method Workflow (Part 1)</strong></p>
            <p>The questions, methods, and outputs of the seven stages:</p>

            <table style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.table }}">
              <thead>
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.headRow }}">
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Stage</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Question Addressed</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Method</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Output</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {% for stage in modelDocking.display.pipeline %}
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.row }}">
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ stage.stage }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ stage.question }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ stage.method }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ stage.output }}</td>
                </tr>
                {% endfor %}
              </tbody>
            </table>

            <p><strong>2.4 Rationale for Method Selection</strong></p>
            <p>Our pipeline adopts a physics-based strategy, prioritizing interpretability and reproducibility. ClusPro
              was chosen for fast rigid-body screening across 17 candidates. HADDOCK was selected for its semi-flexible
              refinement, allowing CDR loop flexibility based on our AlphaFold2 pLDDT scores. Finally, GROMACS with the
              Amber99SB-ILDN force field was used to capture the explicit-solvent dynamics, which is essential for
              accurate MM-GBSA calculations. This multi-scale approach ensures that our computational predictions
              provide an actionable roadmap for wet-lab experiments.</p>

            <p><strong>2.5 Gating System Pathways (Path A / B)</strong></p>
            <p>Each pathway follows a unified workflow: Structure preparation &rarr; OPM/PPM membrane alignment
              &rarr; HADDOCK docking &rarr; SPICE analysis &rarr; CHARMM-GUI membrane system &rarr; GROMACS MD
              &rarr; gmx_MMPBSA.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="model-part1" class="content-card">
          <div class="content-text-frame">
            <h2>Part 1: HA &times; Nanobody Docking (Recognition Element Screening)</h2>

            <h3 id="model-part1-workflow">1.1 Workflow Description</h3>
            <p>Scientific logic arrangement: antigen preparation &rarr; nanobody AF2 &rarr; ClusPro initial screening
              &rarr; HADDOCK refinement &rarr; SPICE interface &rarr; GROMACS MD &rarr; MM-GBSA &rarr; positive
              control &rarr; Card Game.</p>

            <h3 id="model-data-targets">1.2 Antigen Structure Preparation</h3>
            <p>We used two antigen states for docking separately to evaluate the effect of antigen treatment on
              binding modes:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Untreated antigen:</strong> downloaded directly from RCSB, retaining the native
                conformation.</li>
              <li><strong>Complex-processed antigen:</strong> HA extracted from antibody&ndash;antigen complexes, with
                antibody chains and glycosylation removed, representing the bound-state conformation.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Processing workflow: remove antibody chains, glycosylation, water molecules, and heteroatoms; split
              HA1/HA2 into separate chains; unify chain IDs.</p>
            <table style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.table }}">
              <thead>
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.headRow }}">
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Antigen</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Strain</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">PDB ID</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Source</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Resolution</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Retained Chains</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Status</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {% for antigen in modelDocking.display.antigens %}
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.row }}">
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ antigen.name }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ antigen.strain }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ antigen.pdbId }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ antigen.source }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ antigen.resolution }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ antigen.chainMap }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ antigen.status }}</td>
                </tr>
                {% endfor %}
              </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3 id="model-data-nanobodies">1.3 Nanobody AlphaFold2 Prediction</h3>
            <p>The 17 nanobodies are 114&ndash;123 aa in length and have no His-tag or linker. Locally deployed
              ColabFold 1.6.2 was used for structure prediction. The predicted structures of the
              {{ modelDocking.funnel.candidatesRefined }} candidates that entered refinement are self-hosted and shown
              in the card game below (single chain, produced by a structure-prediction model, not an experimental
              structure); scoring and refinement provide citable values only for these
              {{ modelDocking.funnel.candidatesRefined }} (R1aB6, R2aF9, R1aA5, R2bE8, R2bD9).</p>

            <p>Nanobodies with CDR3 pLDDT &lt; 65 were set as flexible in HADDOCK:</p>
            <table style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.table }}">
              <thead>
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.headRow }}">
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Nanobody</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Average pLDDT</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">CDR3 pLDDT</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Assessment</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {% for row in modelDocking.display.nanobodyPredictions %}
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.row }}">
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.id }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.avg }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.cdr3 }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.assessment }}</td>
                </tr>
                {% endfor %}
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <!-- Naming: the source document writes these IDs with an infix X (R1aXA5, R2bXD9, ...); the X-less
                 form is used uniformly on this page pending lab verification (open item MQ-1). -->
            <figure style="margin:1.5rem 0; text-align:center;">
              <img src="../static/image/dry-lab/model/model-fig-1-3-plddt.png" alt="Figure 2. Bar chart of average pLDDT (blue) and CDR3 pLDDT (red) for the 17 candidate nanobodies; the dashed line marks the CDR3 pLDDT 65 threshold below which a candidate was set as flexible in HADDOCK" loading="lazy" width="1268" height="552" style="max-width:100%; height:auto; border:1px solid #ddd;">
              <figcaption style="margin-top:.5rem; color:#666; font-size:.9rem;">Figure 2. Average and CDR3 pLDDT of the 17 candidate nanobodies from structure prediction (computational prediction results)</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <h3 id="model-screening">1.4 ClusPro Rigid Docking</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Scoring function:</strong> {{ modelDocking.scoring.cluspro.formula }}.</li>
              <li><strong>Specificity index:</strong> {{ modelDocking.scoring.specificityIndex.formula }}. {{ modelDocking.scoring.specificityIndex.note }}</li>
              <li><strong>Positive control:</strong> re-docking of the experimentally resolved 9VH4 complex serves as the control for the docking pipeline.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>Stage 1 (untreated antigen, top 5). A more negative specificity index indicates that the candidate is
              more biased toward H1N1 relative to other subtypes:</p>

            <figure style="margin:1.5rem 0; text-align:center;">
              <img src="../static/image/dry-lab/model/model-cluspro-screen.webp" alt="Figure 3. Left: ClusPro scores of the 17 candidates against H1N1, H3N2, H5N1 (weighted score; more negative means stronger binding); Right: average specificity advantage of each candidate against H1N1, with the red flag marking the most specific one" loading="lazy" width="1400" height="693" style="max-width:100%; height:auto; border:1px solid #ddd;">
              <figcaption style="margin-top:.5rem; color:#666; font-size:.9rem;">Figure 3. ClusPro scores and average specificity advantage of the 17 candidates: left, scores against the three influenza A HA antigens; right, specificity advantage (more negative = more specific, red flag marks the best candidate) (computational prediction results)</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p>The {{ modelDocking.funnel.candidatesRefined }} refined candidates' scores and specificity indices under the untreated antigen:</p>
            <table style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.table }}">
              <thead>
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.headRow }}">
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Nanobody</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">H1N1</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">H3N2</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">H5N1</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Specificity Index</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Conclusion</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {% for row in modelDocking.tables.untreated %}
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.row }}">
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.id }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.H1N1 }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.H3N2 }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.H5N1 }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.index }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.outcome }}</td>
                </tr>
                {% endfor %}
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>The ranking is R1aB6 &gt; R2bD9 &gt; R2bE8 &gt; R1aA5 &gt; R2aF9 (from most negative to least negative specificity index).</p>

            <p>Stage 3 (cross-reactivity assessment). Cross-reactivity risk is expressed as the average binding energy
              of candidates against unrelated viral antigens (SARS, HIV1, RSVF); a more negative value indicates
              stronger binding and higher cross-reactivity risk:</p>
            <table style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.table }}">
              <thead>
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.headRow }}">
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Nanobody</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">SARS</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">HIV1</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">RSVF</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Mean Binding Energy</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Cross-reactivity Risk</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {% for row in modelDocking.tables.cross %}
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.row }}">
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.id }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.SARS }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.HIV1 }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.RSVF }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.mean }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.risk }}</td>
                </tr>
                {% endfor %}
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <!-- MQ-7: the source document has two additional 17-antibody charts (processed antigen, cross-reactivity) placed after 5.3.2 / 5.3.3,
                 whose candidate-number cross-reference tables contradict the numbering in the screening figure, so they cannot be mapped row-by-row to this section's tables; this page therefore does not cite those two charts,
                 and the related results are presented only in tables. See md/10-一手分析.md §5.1, §6. -->

            <h3 id="model-haddock">1.5 HADDOCK 2.5 Semi-Flexible Refinement</h3>
            <p>Active residues: HA1 (25, 45&ndash;49, 305&ndash;307, 332), HA2 (361&ndash;364, 381&ndash;385, 388,
              391&ndash;392, 395&ndash;396, 399), and nanobody CDR residues. Results after semi-flexible refinement
              of the {{ modelDocking.funnel.candidatesRefined }} refined candidates against H1N1 HA:</p>
            <table style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.table }}">
              <thead>
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.headRow }}">
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Nanobody</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">HADDOCK score</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Cluster Size</th>
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">BSA (Å²)</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {% for row in modelDocking.tables.haddock %}
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.row }}">
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.id }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.score }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.clusterSize }}</td>
                  <td style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.td }}">{{ row.bsa }}</td>
                </tr>
                {% endfor %}
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>The ranking is R1aB6 &gt; R2aF9 &gt; R1aA5 &gt; R2bE8 &gt; R2bD9. Plotting the two metrics together shows they do not always move in the same direction:</p>

            <p>The best: R1aB6-H1N1 Cluster 5, score &minus;93.2, RMSD 0.7 &Aring;, Zscore &minus;1.9, Elec
              &minus;319.1.</p>

            <figure style="margin:1.5rem 0; text-align:center;">
              <img src="../static/image/dry-lab/model/model-specificity-vs-haddock.webp" alt="Figure 4. Combined view: horizontal axis is the ClusPro specificity index (more negative is better), vertical axis is the HADDOCK score (more negative is better); R1aB6 falls in the optimal lower-left region, while R2aF9 falls in the right region with poor specificity" loading="lazy" width="1269" height="952" style="max-width:100%; height:auto; border:1px solid #ddd;">
              <figcaption style="margin-top:.5rem; color:#666; font-size:.9rem;">Figure 4. Combined view of the two metrics: horizontal axis is the ClusPro specificity index, vertical axis is the HADDOCK score, both better when more negative; R1aB6 sits at the optimal position in both directions (computational prediction results)</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <figure style="margin:1.5rem 0; text-align:center;">
              <img src="../static/image/dry-lab/model/model-haddock-bars.webp" alt="Figure 5. Bar chart of HADDOCK scores for the 5 candidates, with score, BSA, and cluster size N annotated on the bars" loading="lazy" width="1269" height="793" style="max-width:100%; height:auto; border:1px solid #ddd;">
              <figcaption style="margin-top:.5rem; color:#666; font-size:.9rem;">Figure 5. HADDOCK score, buried surface area (BSA), and cluster size (N) for the {{ modelDocking.funnel.candidatesRefined }} candidates (computational prediction results)</figcaption>
              <!-- The source document's own figure plots BSA 2634 Å² (R1aA5) and 2539 Å² (R2bE8), while its table —
                   and the raw HADDOCK cluster stat files behind the card game — record 2630.4 Å² and 2619.9 Å².
                   The table above follows the document table and the raw stat files. -->
            </figure>

            <h3 id="model-interface">1.6 PyMOL + SPICE Interface Analysis</h3>
            <p>Interface analysis targets the best combination, R1aB6 with H1N1 HA. PyMOL gives 30 interface residues on the HA side ({{ modelDocking.interface.haSide.ranges | join(', ') }}) and 24 residues on the nanobody side (CDR1 27–37, CDR2 44–65, CDR3 101–104). SPICE gives the following counts of interaction types for the two best clusters of the refinement:</p>
            <table style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.table }}">
              <thead>
                <tr style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.headRow }}">
                  <th style="{{ modelDocking.tableStyle.th }}">Interaction Type</th>
