            <p><strong>Detailed Analysis of Failure Causes</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>hM1Dq + Gpa1 total CHARMM-GUI failure: The Gpa1 trimer was misidentified as a continuous long chain;
                a 25 &Aring; long bond appeared between residues 512&ndash;513; minimization Fmax = 16839, ultimately
                Exit 139; fix: truncate residues 461&ndash;512 and switch to a single chain.</li>
              <li>Accidental incorporation of CHL1 cholesterol: In one CHARMM-GUI run, ergosterol was mistakenly
                replaced with cholesterol (360 CHL1); corrected after reconstruction.</li>
              <li>Membrane system too large: hM1Dq + Gpa1 total has 584,325 atoms and cannot run MD stably under WSL;
                hM1Dq + Gpa&alpha; single chain has approximately 850,000 atoms, estimated to take 2&ndash;3 weeks,
                listed as Future Work.</li>
              <li>Ste2p + Gpa1 coupling was not run: listed as Future Work.</li>
              <li>The Ste2p + MT1_5 complex was simulated without its downstream Gpa1 coupling partner, which may
                affect the conformational sampling of the intracellular loops.</li>
            </ul>

            <p><strong>Membrane MD validation of G protein coupling</strong></p>
            <p>This work has completed membrane MD validation of two gating interfaces, hM1Dq&ndash;MT1 and
              Ste2p&ndash;MT1_5. For the G protein coupling interfaces (hM1Dq&ndash;Gpa1 and Ste2p&ndash;Gpa1), we have
              completed HADDOCK semi-flexible refinement and SPICE interface analysis and identified key coupling
              residues. Because the complete Gpa1 trimer membrane system (approximately 850,000 atoms) requires
              approximately 2&ndash;3 weeks to complete 100 ns MD under the available computational resources, we list
              it as next-stage work. The current HADDOCK data (Gpa&alpha; single-chain score = &minus;149.4, superior to
              total = &minus;131.7) already support the conclusion that the coupling interface is primarily mediated by
              G&alpha;.</p>

