            <h3 id="model-conclusion">Conclusion</h3>
            <p>The modeling work of EchoYeast embodies the core value of the iGEM computational-first / model-guided
              strategy. Starting from 17 candidate nanobodies, through a five-stage multiscale workflow of ClusPro
              &rarr; HADDOCK &rarr; PyMOL/SPICE &rarr; GROMACS &rarr; gmx_MMPBSA, combined with positive control 9VH4
              redocking validation, we successfully converged on the optimal candidate R1aB6, identified the key hotspot
              ARG-B:31, and proposed a testable hypothesis, the R31A mutant.</p>
            <p>In parallel, we advanced the modeling of two gating systems:</p>
            <p>Path A: hM1Dq + MT1 (&Delta;TOTAL = &minus;40.37 &plusmn; 1.27 kcal/mol, 50 interface residues)</p>
            <p>Path B: Ste2p + MT1_5 (&Delta;TOTAL = &minus;31.43 &plusmn; 3.46 kcal/mol, 63 interface residues)</p>
            <p>Both have completed 100 ns membrane MD, MM-GBSA, and per-residue decomposition, and through SPICE
              interface analysis revealed the specific interaction networks, providing atomic-level structural support
              for the whole-cell biosensor of EchoYeast.</p>
            <p>The value of the model lies not in replacing wet-lab experiments, but in providing precise targets and
              prioritization for them &mdash; this is precisely the direction advocated by the iGEM Best Model Award.
              All simulation inputs, parameter files, and analysis scripts are available at the
              <a href="https://github.com/vincenzorui0805-glitch/EchoYeast-Modeling" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub repository</a>,
              ensuring full reproducibility of the computational workflow.</p>
            <p><strong>Note:</strong> The repository is public. If you encounter network issues accessing GitHub, the
              source code and parameter files are also available in the iGEM Registry Part pages or upon request.</p>
